// Home Lab — Phase 08 escalation grant (ADR-024).
//
// Installed to /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-homelab-bot.rules
//
// WHY POLKIT AND NOT SUDO
//
//   The brief specified a sudoers rule. That cannot work here, and the reason
//   was found by testing rather than reasoning:
//
//     $ setpriv --no-new-privs sudo -n true
//     sudo: The "no new privileges" flag is set, which prevents sudo from
//           running as root.
//
//   homelab-telegram-bot.service sets NoNewPrivileges=yes, which is one of the
//   strongest properties Phase 07 established. sudo is setuid, so it is refused
//   outright under that flag. Using sudo would have meant REMOVING the
//   hardening in order to add an escalation -- exactly backwards.
//
//   polkit needs no setuid. systemctl asks PID 1 over D-Bus, and polkit decides
//   inside PID 1. NoNewPrivileges stays on.
//
//   It is also safer to get wrong. A malformed sudoers file breaks sudo
//   entirely, on a node whose admin has no console and no other escalation
//   path. A malformed polkit rule DENIES. The failure modes are not comparable.
//
// SCOPE
//
//   One user, one unit, one verb. No wildcards anywhere.
//
//   A wildcard here would be the same mistake as `systemctl restart *` in
//   sudoers: it would reach ssh, tailscaled and systemd-networkd -- every
//   service whose loss costs access to this machine.
//
// AUDIT -- and a correction, because the first version of this file was wrong
//
//   The claim here used to be "polkit logs every authorisation decision". It
//   does not. Measured on this node:
//
//     denials  -> polkitd logs "FAILED to authenticate to gain authorization
//                 for action org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units ...
//                 (owned by unix-user:homelab-bot)"
//     grants   -> NOTHING. Zero lines.
//
//   A polkit.log() call was added here to cover that and produced no output
//   either; polkitd filters it out at its default log level, and turning on
//   polkit debugging globally to record one rule is not a trade worth making.
//   The dead call has been removed rather than left in place implying a
//   guarantee it never provided.
//
//   An audit trail that records refusals but not approvals is half an audit
//   trail, and it is the wrong half: for a privileged action, the successes are
//   what you most want to be able to reconstruct.
//
//   The second independent record is therefore SYSTEMD's, not polkit's:
//
//     Sep 09 19:53:09 systemd[1]: Stopping chrony.service ...
//     Sep 09 19:53:09 systemd[1]: Started chrony.service ...
//
//   That is PID 1, a different process from the bot with a different failure
//   mode, and it records the action actually happening rather than someone's
//   intention to perform it. Together with the bot's own log -- which names the
//   requesting user, the executor and the outcome -- that is two independent
//   records. polkit contributes a third, for denials only.

polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
    if (action.id == "org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units" &&
        subject.user == "homelab-bot" &&
        action.lookup("unit") == "chrony.service" &&
        action.lookup("verb") == "restart") {

        // No polkit.log() here: it is filtered out at the default log level and
        // produced nothing when tested. See the AUDIT note above -- systemd's
        // own record of the unit starting is the durable evidence of a grant.
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }

    // Everything else falls through to the system default, which for
    // manage-units is auth_admin -- i.e. denied for an unprivileged,
    // non-interactive service account. Fail closed by omission.
});
